Yes. The clean way to connect your HTML web resource to your Copilot Agent is to place a secure agent bridge between them.

Do not put the Copilot/Direct Line secret inside the HTML file.

Final connection architecture
Dynamics 365 Opportunity form
→ HTML web resource
→ Secure Agent Bridge / API
→ Copilot Studio Agent
→ Agent tools/flows
→ Dataverse staging + Opportunity Product Lines
→ Agent returns JSON response
→ HTML displays result

Microsoft notes that Copilot Studio uses the Bot Framework Direct Line channel to connect a web page or app to an agent, and Direct Line secrets/tokens are used for secured access. That secret should be protected server-side, not exposed in browser JavaScript.

Step 1 — Make sure your Copilot Agent is ready

In Copilot Studio, confirm your agent has the right tools:

CTP Vendor Quote Agent

Minimum tools:

Tool	Purpose
CTP_ReadVendorQuoteFile	Reads PDF, Excel, DOCX, etc.
CTP_GenerateVendorQuotePayload	Generates the normalized payload
CTP_MatchVendorQuoteLines	Maps quote lines to Dynamics products
CTP_CreateVendorQuoteStagingFromPayload_Agent	Creates staging records
CTP_ApproveAndCreateOpportunityProducts	Creates final Opportunity Product Lines

Agent flows used as tools must use When an agent calls the flow and Respond to the agent, and they must respond in real time, not asynchronously.

Step 2 — Publish the agent to a custom app channel

In Copilot Studio:

CTP Vendor Quote Agent
→ Channels
→ Custom app / mobile app / Direct Line-based channel
→ Enable secured access
→ Get Direct Line configuration

You need the channel details because your custom HTML UI will not use the normal Copilot chat canvas. It will communicate with the agent through a custom app connection. Microsoft’s custom app documentation says starting a conversation with a Copilot Studio agent requires a Direct Line token.

Step 3 — Create a secure Agent Bridge

This is the missing piece.

The bridge can be one of these:

Bridge option	Recommendation
Azure Function	Best and cleanest
Dataverse Custom API + plugin	Good Dataverse-native option
Power Automate HTTP flow	Fast MVP, less ideal for secrets
Small Node/.NET API	Good if you want full control

For your case, I recommend:

Azure Function Agent Bridge

The bridge will:

1. Receive request from HTML web resource
2. Validate the Dynamics user/request
3. Get or create Direct Line token/conversation
4. Send structured activity to Copilot Agent
5. Wait for agent response
6. Return clean JSON to HTML
Step 4 — Your HTML sends this request to the bridge

When the user clicks:

Ask Agent to Process Quote

The HTML should send:

{
  "operation": "extract_map_preview",
  "opportunity": {
    "id": "CURRENT_OPPORTUNITY_GUID",
    "name": "Current Opportunity"
  },
  "file": {
    "fileName": "vendor quote.pdf",
    "fileReference": "SharePoint or Dataverse file reference",
    "mimeType": "application/pdf"
  },
  "hints": {
    "vendorName": "optional",
    "userInstruction": "optional"
  }
}

Then when the user approves and creates records, HTML sends:

{
  "operation": "approve_create_products",
  "opportunity": {
    "id": "CURRENT_OPPORTUNITY_GUID",
    "name": "Current Opportunity"
  },
  "approval": {
    "userApproved": true
  },
  "currentPayload": {
    "header": {},
    "lines": []
  }
}

The key point: the final payload sent back includes the user’s saved edits and per-line approvals.

